WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 75: Hot Springs: The Gangsters' Neutral Haven and the Bathhouse Row

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:14.560
[Rebecca] Nestled deep in the Wachita Mountains of southwest Arkansas lies a narrow valley steaming with naturally boiling mineral water.

4
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:20.400
[Rebecca] By the early 20th century, Hot Springs was world-renowned for its lavish marble bathhouses.

5
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.040
[Rebecca] But behind the steam and neoclassical colonnades lay an open secret.

6
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.080
[Rebecca] Hot Springs was the undisputed neutral sanctuary of the American underworld.

7
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:48.320
[Rebecca] Protected by a compliant mayor and local police, deadly rivals like Al Capone, Lucky Luciano, Oni Madden, and Mayor Lansky vacationed side by side, drank champagne, wagered millions in illegal casinos, and soaked in the thermal springs without firing a single shot.

8
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.960
[Rebecca] George brings us the colorful story of America's mobster spa.

9
00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:03.920
[George] For thousands of years before European exploration, indigenous peoples of the Wachita Basin regarded the steaming hot springs as sacred ground.

10
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:17.760
[George] More than 40 natural thermal springs gushed from the sandstone bluffs, producing nearly a million gallons of odorless, mineral-rich water heated by subterranean geothermal energy to 143 degrees Fahrenheit.

11
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.920
[George] Native tribes established an unwritten treaty of perpetual truce.

12
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:28.400
[George] No weapons were permitted in the valley, allowing warring warriors to bathe and heal together in mutual peace.

13
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:45.920
[George] When Spanish conquistador Hernando de Soto passed through in 1541, he marveled at the steaming valley, and in 1832, Congress took the unprecedented step of designating hot springs as the nation's first Federal Reserve, predating Yellowstone by 40 years.

14
00:01:45.920 --> 00:02:01.640
[George] As the railroad reached Arkansas in the late 19th century, grand brick and marble bathhouses arose along Central Avenue, turning Bathhouse Row into a premier health resort for wealthy travelers seeking cures for arthritis, rheumatism, and fatigue.

15
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:18.280
[George] The thermal waters attracted physicians, socialites, and professional athletes alike, with major league baseball teams establishing their annual spring training camps in the valley so legendary players like Babe Ruth could sweat out winter excess in the opulent thermal baths.

16
00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:32.680
[George] The architecture of Bath House Row was a breathtaking display of Victorian and Mediterranean revival styles, boasting stained glass skylights, imported Italian marble massage tables, and porcelain hydrotherapy needle baths.

17
00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:44.360
[George] Wealthy patrons paid handsomely for full treatment courses, reclining in individual zinc tubs while attendants wrapped them in steaming hot towels and administered curative Swedish massages.

18
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:50.440
[George] Believing the sulfurous minerals could dissolve kidney stones and reverse the ailments of old age.

19
00:02:50.440 --> 00:03:05.080
[George] Doctors from across the United States prescribed 30-day treatment regimens in hot springs, establishing specialized clinics along Central Avenue where patients consumed measured doses of thermal water to treat digestive and metabolic disorders.

20
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:13.240
[George] The transformation of hot springs from a quiet health spa into an underworld paradise was the handiwork of one man, Mayor Leo P.

21
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:14.440
[George] McLaughlin.

22
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:25.640
[George] Elected in 1927, McLaughlin was a flamboyant, dapper dandy who wore custom tailored suits, diamond stickpins, and drove a horse drawn carriage through town.

23
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.680
[George] McLaughlin recognized that wealthy visitors wanted more than hot mineral baths.

24
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.640
[George] They wanted high stakes nighttime excitement.

25
00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:44.360
[George] He organized an invincible political machine, utilizing poll tax manipulation, compliant police chiefs, and hand picked judges to run hot springs with absolute authority.

26
00:03:44.480 --> 00:04:04.480
[George] McLaughlin instituted a simple municipal compact, casino gambling, horse racing, and illegal speakeasies would operate in wide open defiance of Arkansas state law, provided that casino operators paid substantial monthly tributes to the city machine and strictly avoided street violence or armed robbery.

27
00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:23.840
[George] Under McLaughlin's benevolent protection, Hot Springs blossomed into an oasis of illicit luxury, where neon lit casinos like the Southern Club and the Belvedere rivaled the finest gambling palaces of Monte Carlo and Havana, complete with crystal chandeliers, French roulette wheels, and live big bands.

28
00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:36.640
[George] McLaughlin controlled every aspect of municipal government from his wood-paneled office in City Hall, ensuring that visiting federal agents were shadowed by local plainclothes detectives from the moment their trains arrived.

29
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:52.400
[George] High-end illegal casinos were disguised behind unassuming storefronts on Central Avenue, where patrons pressed discreet brass buzzers to pass through steel-reinforced double doors into sumptuous gaming parlors complete with crystal chandeliers and complimentary caviar.

30
00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:58.560
[George] No gangland figure loved hot springs more passionately than Chicago crime kingpin Al Capone.

31
00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:08.800
[George] Beginning in the 1920s, Capone made regular pilgrimages to the valley, traveling south in a private luxury railroad car accompanied by bodyguards and entourage.

32
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:21.920
[George] Capone made the Arlington Hotel his personal headquarters, reserving the entire fourth floor, including Suite 442, which featured private thermal mineral water piped directly into his porcelain tub.

33
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:37.080
[George] To the local citizens and elevator operators of Hot Springs, Capone was an exceedingly generous, courteous gentleman who tipped $100 bills, paid off mortgages for impoverished widows, and bought baseball uniforms for local schoolboys.

34
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:48.840
[George] Capone spent sunny afternoons practicing his swing at the Hot Springs Country Club, strolled down Central Avenue waving to admirers, and spent his evenings rolling dice at the Southern Club.

35
00:05:48.840 --> 00:06:03.240
[George] When rival Chicago gangsters attempted to follow him south, McLaughlin's police chief met them at the train station, politely informing them that weapons and turf wars were strictly forbidden in hot springs on pain of instant deportation.

36
00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:15.400
[George] Capone's armored Cadillac, fitted with bulletproof glass and hidden gun racks, was a familiar sight parked outside the Arlington Hotel or idling in front of the Southern Club on humid summer nights.

37
00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:28.120
[George] Chicago mobsters valued Hot Springs because it was one of the few places in North America where rival factions could enjoy open-air dinners without constantly watching the doors for Hitman wielding Thompson submachine guns.

38
00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:37.000
[George] While Capone was a frequent guest, another legendary underworld titan made Hot Springs his permanent home, Oni the Killer Madden.

39
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:44.440
[George] Madden was the feared former boss of Manhattan's West Side Gophers and the proprietor of Harlem's world famous cotton club.

40
00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:54.520
[George] Paroled from Sing Sing prison in the early 1930s under federal condition that he leave New York forever, Madden relocated to the quiet mountain air of Hot Springs.

41
00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:59.640
[George] Instead of retirement, Madden became the elder statesman of the underworld spa.

42
00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:11.560
[George] He married Agnes Dowd, the charming daughter of a respected local postmaster, built an elegant stone cottage, and invested heavily in local charity, churches, and civic parks.

43
00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:21.120
[George] Madden became the indispensable underworld diplomat, mediating disputes between the Chicago outfit and New York's five families from his booth at the Southern Club.

44
00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:36.400
[George] Under Madden's gentlemanly guidance, Hot Springs maintained its sacred status as neutral ground, where mobsters from across the nation could negotiate multimillion dollar agreements over lobster thermidor and mineral water without fear of assassination.

45
00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:40.480
[George] Madden's influence extended far beyond the gambling dens.

46
00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:50.160
[George] He invested heavily in local hardware stores, pharmacies and lumberyards, earning the genuine respect and affection of rural Arkansans who knew him simply as Mr.

47
00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:50.960
[George] Oney.

48
00:07:50.960 --> 00:08:01.600
[George] Madden walked his pet terrier along the mountain promenade each morning, greeting townspeople by name and quietly slipping cash envelopes to families struggling with medical bills.

49
00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:05.760
[George] Madden was a devout Roman Catholic who attended Mass weekly at St.

50
00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:14.720
[George] Mary of the Springs, quietly donating thousands of dollars to parish charities and parochial school scholarships without ever seeking public acknowledgement.

51
00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:24.160
[George] Hot Springs' reputation as an untouchable sanctuary was put to its greatest test in the spring of 1936, when New York's special prosecutor Thomas E.

52
00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:28.000
[George] Dewey launched his historic campaign against organized crime.

53
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:34.720
[George] Dewey indicted Charles Lucky Luciano, the undisputed architect of the modern American Mafia Commission.

54
00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:47.120
[George] Tipped off by associates, Luciano fled New York and boarded an overnight train directly for Hot Springs, believing that McLaughlin's machine and Arkansas extradition laws would shield him indefinitely.

55
00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:54.640
[George] Luciano lived luxuriously at the Arlington Hotel, guarded by local detectives and gambling nightly at the casinos.

56
00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:03.160
[George] But Dewey dispatched a determined squad of New York detectives who tracked Luciano to the Hot Springs race track and cornered him on Central Avenue.

57
00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:13.080
[George] When an Arkansas County judge attempted to release Luciano on a suspicious $50,000 bond arranged by McLaughlin, federal authorities and Arkansas Governor J.

58
00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:18.200
[George] Marion Futtrell intervened, surrounding the courthouse with armed state troopers.

59
00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:31.240
[George] Luciano was extradited back to Manhattan in handcuffs, convicted of 62 counts of compulsory prostitution, and sentenced to 30 to 50 years in prison, proving that even Hot Springs had its limits.

60
00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:41.160
[George] Dewey's team of Manhattan prosecutors had spent months documenting Luciano's criminal syndicates, determined to dismantle the National Mafia Commission at its peak.

61
00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:54.680
[George] The legal battle in Arkansas generated national newspaper headlines, exposing the shadowy connections between Southern political machines and northern organized crime families that had operated with impunity for over a decade.

62
00:09:54.680 --> 00:10:07.880
[George] The golden age of Hot Springs gambling began to crumble following World War II, sparked by returning soldiers who had fought overseas for democracy only to find their hometown ruled by corrupt machine politicians.

63
00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:17.160
[George] Led by a decorated Marine Corps veteran named Sid McMath, the GI Civic League organized to challenge McLaughlin's stranglehold on the city.

64
00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:30.600
[George] In the 1946 municipal elections, veteran candidates fought gun-toting machine thugs at polling places, seized ballot boxes to prevent tampering, and swept McLaughlin's corrupt cronies from office.

65
00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:43.160
[George] Sid McMath was elected prosecuting attorney and later governor of Arkansas, pledging to enforce state gambling bans and dismantle the corrupt alliances between police and illegal casino syndicates.

66
00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:59.440
[George] Yet even after the GI revolt, the lucrative lure of the casinos proved remarkably resilient, as gaming tables continued to operate behind discrete velvet curtains and buzzer systems, surviving multiple police raids and state crackdowns for another two decades.

67
00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:14.880
[George] Sid McMath was a charismatic trial attorney whose courtroom rhetoric captivated rural juries, presenting the struggle against illegal gambling not merely as a matter of municipal law, but as a moral crusade to restore honest democracy to Arkansas.

68
00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:26.000
[George] Veteran organizations banded together to form volunteer poll watching squads, refusing to be intimidated by armed machine enforcers during heated election night confrontations.

69
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:38.240
[George] The final death knell for illegal gambling in Hot Springs sounded in 1967, when Arkansas elected Winthrop Rockefeller, the state's first Republican governor since Reconstruction.

70
00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:50.240
[George] Rockefeller refused to compromise with local interests, deploying a task force of state troopers equipped with sledgehammers to execute coordinated midnight raids on every major casino in the valley.

71
00:11:50.240 --> 00:12:02.560
[George] Over several chaotic nights, troopers smashed roulette wheels, confiscated millions in cash, and burned hundreds of greenfelt craps tables in giant bonfires behind police headquarters.

72
00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.000
[George] The glittering era of gangster glamour was over forever.

73
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:21.040
[George] Today, Bathhouse Row is preserved as Hot Springs National Park, where visitors stroll past the ornate marble facades of the Fordyce and Buckstaff bathhouses, sipping pure mineral water that still bubbles from the earth at 140 degrees.

74
00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:36.760
[George] The memory of Al Capone's cigar smoke and Oni Madden's quiet diplomacy lingers in the historic lobbies of Central Avenue, an unforgettable chapter in American folklore when the mobsters of the world laid down their guns to take the waters together in the hills of Arkansas.

75
00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:49.000
[George] Today, the former casinos on Central Avenue have been transformed into art galleries, boutique hotels, and microbreweries that utilize the mountain spring water for brewing award-winning craft ales.

76
00:12:49.000 --> 00:13:05.880
[George] The steaming fountains in front of the Morris and Superior bathhouses continue to draw millions of travelers each year, standing as peaceful reminders of the eternal geothermal fires that outlived all the gangsters, politicians, and high-rolling gamblers of the Gilded Age.

77
00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:14.600
[Rebecca] Hot springs proved that even the deadliest underworld empires could agree on the soothing power of hot mineral water and high stakes roulette.

78
00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:15.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

79
00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:31.800
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey to the rugged ridges of West Virginia for the epic coal miners' rebellion at the Battle of Blair Mountain.

